#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3977d627-65bc-5f36-8c25-7d47b515f383" :question/math-academy-id "e-3566" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=9x^4$ and $q(x)=3x^4$, what is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105564 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/id #uuid "88c456d9-e5d3-5b33-a847-03f201751c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-36" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(-12x^{12}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y=x^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^{12} = \\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-12$ causes $y=-12x^{12}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-12{x}^{12})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/id #uuid "551f2aa0-8589-5725-abe6-de1b7eca0327" :question/math-academy-id "e-37" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=x^3$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty } x^3 = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "969d5f07-c5ce-5601-b2de-0d23eb15809b" :question/math-academy-id "e-39" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow -\\infty} (44-3y^4-9y^{12}) \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }(44-3{y}^{4}-9{y}^{12}) & ={lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }(-9{y}^{12}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }({y}^{12}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7740ff6c-f994-55ad-a058-c084db22a725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7740ff6c-f994-55ad-a058-c084db22a725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec63b2c8-7a90-5b2e-aad9-f70f82165d1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7987b888-d840-5cef-9095-39b390eecef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-300758" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36e1b866-824a-58a7-814f-c1722a1f56f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e1b866-824a-58a7-814f-c1722a1f56f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295576} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a3d9c8-2289-5234-90ff-247924256a69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7a2c77c-6afe-560a-891d-7d57e249dc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-300764" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-3{x}^{5}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-3{x}^{5})=-3{x}^{7}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bce09fa5-f711-56c7-921c-dd09238f7098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce09fa5-f711-56c7-921c-dd09238f7098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562064} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5c298f-1969-5828-93b7-b6ec5738de4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc7d6aa1-177c-5e42-a678-f62831d377fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-336864" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{8}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{8}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{8})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc3a515-ec82-55c9-851c-75e95ba6146e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbc3a515-ec82-55c9-851c-75e95ba6146e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562129} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ba131f5-3a7e-5441-a963-a775a6a778f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be0311ba-56bf-5adc-9620-b550fa934c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-336941" :question/problem "If $u(x)=14{x}^{11}$ and $v(x)=-7{x}^{11},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{11}}{(-7{x}^{11})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e942e0-df6b-5067-9ec2-d663cc2809a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309307} {:answer/id #uuid "2d260467-07cb-5e14-b0cb-436d603f9f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d260467-07cb-5e14-b0cb-436d603f9f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561698} :answer-field/id #uuid "8065c2cb-0482-5544-bb13-5ce53d60d55d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d098104-b590-5e0a-abfc-234f5e11d03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337034" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{5}-7{x}^{3}+2x-9)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{5}-7{x}^{3}+2x-9) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359663eb-d2c6-5a5c-ac88-84513a628e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319557} {:answer/id #uuid "41c85901-286a-56c4-818b-25a56eab9866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319589} {:answer/id #uuid "a906fbea-bddc-5793-8448-df6e99c46520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319898} {:answer/id #uuid "a97ce742-6332-5b74-8d58-2a9c4f1c1906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319899} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7d65fc-d75a-5d39-8d9d-808c59a1c861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c85901-286a-56c4-818b-25a56eab9866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319589} :answer-field/id #uuid "136c60b5-ff17-5376-a897-abf2b41e3569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57808d0b-eb9d-5a9c-ab3c-43a379853a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-35122" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}+4).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}+4) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0495d9ff-1222-5097-80a1-d2c8e3522b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561594} {:answer/id #uuid "25578ecd-9194-5319-a63e-979c98387470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561683} {:answer/id #uuid "2d43a0d1-25dc-5b09-b9c3-641cd88bdb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561699} {:answer/id #uuid "71026400-c6fb-5c50-8a9e-8626cef1e343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561866} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc8aa42-2cbe-56b2-8cd8-103a778bb6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25578ecd-9194-5319-a63e-979c98387470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561683} :answer-field/id #uuid "304ab48c-09a2-55aa-ab3e-0e07f921b28b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b520f9a-26c5-5349-931d-9a87c3770c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-35612" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=q(x)=-3{x}^{3},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{3}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(-3{x}^{3})}^{3}\\cdot {(-3{x}^{3})}^{2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-27{x}^{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{6}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((-27\\cdot 9){x}^{9+6}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-243\\cdot {x}^{15}) \\\\  & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{15}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-243$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-243{x}^{15})=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d27e1a7-6910-59ed-bd3d-2ff6d6328c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561622} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ae8bd4-10e7-5e5c-99f9-4901d6b82be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562000} {:answer/id #uuid "b8349b7a-9285-555f-9276-3a5603cf4fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562046} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bda25e-5bda-5994-87a1-09a475eb1182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562119} {:answer/id #uuid "f9936a72-9fad-5b49-893b-5a1b6c454bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9936a72-9fad-5b49-893b-5a1b6c454bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562200} :answer-field/id #uuid "785495d1-b945-5b35-848c-1112df719677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e88bf6aa-56d0-5447-b3d1-8bec7e8eb9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99289" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=5{x}^{7},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3{x}^{2}\\cdot 5{x}^{7}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((3\\cdot 5){x}^{2+7}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(15\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $15$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(15{x}^{9})=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3987b6a8-c74c-528b-9cc2-085124cd1b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561727} {:answer/id #uuid "5eab2f79-eb6b-55d8-8015-08218199e422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561813} {:answer/id #uuid "75cc82e2-0552-5e05-afa6-010934ed1ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561874} {:answer/id #uuid "c246e2da-00be-5471-a59a-83ec193748b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562071} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a69e0a-a9c5-5723-a6b9-51c36e031c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0a69e0a-a9c5-5723-a6b9-51c36e031c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562102} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee8ef262-a304-54cc-b9b6-e3e50ca4de07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2f7fefa-e39c-5728-9247-538da8dbc2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99294" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=6{x}^{9}$ and $q(x)=15{x}^{7},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (15{x}^{7})=15{x}^{9}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)$ and $p(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15{x}^{9}}{6{x}^{9}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
